  "worked_solution": "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne {90}^{\u2218}+n\\cdot {180}^{\u2218},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $2\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(3x-{60}^{\u2218}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x-{60}^{\u2218}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x-{60}^{\u2218}\\ne {90}^{\u2218}+n\\cdot {180}^{\u2218}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{60}^{\u2218} & \\ne {90}^{\u2218}+n\\cdot {180}^{\u2218} \\\\ 3x & \\ne {90}^{\u2218}+n\\cdot {180}^{\u2218}+{60}^{\u2218} \\\\ 3x & \\ne {150}^{\u2218}+n\\cdot {180}^{\u2218} \\\\ x & \\ne {50}^{\u2218}+n\\cdot {60}^{\u2218}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne {50}^{\u2218}+n\\cdot {60}^{\u2218},$ where $n$ is an integer.",
